Write this client's first home week. The daily tracker reads the text, so the shape below is required.

CLIENT DETAILS:
[CLIENT DETAILS]

HOW TO WRITE IT:

1. Open with 2 to 4 sentences to the client by first name. Mention their goal and the equipment they actually have. Do not mention a coaching week number. NEVER write "Welcome to week", "week N", or a cross-day pointer.

2. Label every day "Day N (Weekday)" with Day 1 = Monday through Day 7 = Sunday. After the colon, use a plain focus: Push, Pull, Legs, Upper, Lower, or Rest. Example: "Day 1 (Monday): Push". Do not put Power, Hypertrophy, or Density in the day title.

3. Pick one split and keep it. Use upper/lower, or push/pull/legs when they train 3 or 6 to 7 days. Training days per week is a hard cap. Leftover days are Rest. One region per day. Never a full-body day or a full-body circuit.
   A Push day is push-ups, shoulder presses, and triceps only. No row or pull-up.
   A Pull day is rows, pull-ups or chin-ups, and biceps curls only. No push-up, bench press, or shoulder press.
   An Upper day may include a press and a row. It has no squat, lunge, or hinge.
   A Lower or Legs day is a squat or split squat, a Romanian deadlift or glute bridge, and a calf raise. No press, row, or curl.
   A core finisher may sit at the end of Main Workout on any day. Do not put the same working exercise on two calendar days in a row.

4. Use only equipment they listed, plus bodyweight. If they have no barbell, do not write a barbell lift. If they have no cable machine, do not write a cable, lat pulldown, leg press, barbell, Smith machine, or hack squat. A band pull is a Resistance Band Row. Allowed home names: Push Up, Knee Push Up, Incline Push Up, Dumbbell Bench Press, Shoulder Press, Dumbbell Row, Resistance Band Row, Pull Up, Chin Up, Goblet Squat, Split Squat, Lunge, Dumbbell Romanian Deadlift, Glute Bridge, Hip Thrust, Calf Raise, Biceps Curl, Triceps Pushdown, Lateral Raise, Plank, Crunch. Do not invent a move. Do not put tempo, pause, or deficit in the exercise name.

5. Under every training day, put these labels on their own lines, in order: Warm-up: then Main Workout: then Post-Workout:. Warm-up is an easy march and joint circles only. Do not put a named lift in the warm-up, and never repeat an exercise that is also in Main Workout. Each exercise name once per day. A dumbbell row and a band row are the same row, so write only one. Write only one bench press. Main Workout is the working lifts, about 5 to 7 of them. Post-Workout is stretches only. Do not add a shared warmup after the week. The daily tracker cannot resolve cross-day references. NEVER write "same as Day 1", "repeat Monday", or "as above". If two days share a session, rewrite the complete exercise list under both headers.

6. Write each working exercise as "Exercise: N sets x M reps. One short sentence in everyday words." Example: "Push Up: 3 sets x 8 reps. Hands under your shoulders, lower your chest to the floor, then press back up." Use a plain letter x. Never 5 or more working sets. Beginners use 3 working sets. Intermediate and advanced use 4 on the main moves and 3 on the smaller ones. Beginners use 8 to 15 reps. On the main moves, write "stop when you could still do 2 more clean reps." Do not write RIR, RDL, hypertrophy, mesocycle, compounds, isolation, or delts.

7. Weave one line for a daily step target and a short sleep note of 7.5 to 9 hours. Do not add a Cardio or Supplements header. Do not write calorie targets. Close with two encouraging sentences. Never use a hyphen, en dash, or em dash. Write "6 to 8" not "6-8".

8. If anything here conflicts with Hard Constraints, obey Hard Constraints.
